Token capacity per minute = 100 000 / 80 = 1 250 requests. The request‑rate limit allows only 1 000 requests, so the request limit is the bottleneck. Over 30 minutes the tier can issue 1 000 × 30 = 30 000 requests. Any additional request would exceed the request‑rate limit, triggering the request‑rate‑exceeded JSON error:
{{
  "error": {{
    "message": "Rate limit exceeded. You have exceeded the maximum number of requests per minute for your tier.",
    "type": "rate_limit_error",
    "code": 429
  }}
}}

First, the token‑consumption limit allows 100 000 tokens ÷ 120 tokens ≈ 833 requests per minute (the fractional part is discarded). The request‑rate limit permits 1 000 requests per minute, so the token limit is tighter. Therefore the tier can handle 833 requests each minute, for a total of 833 × 20 = 16 660 requests over 20 minutes before the token quota is exhausted. When the token quota is exceeded, MaaS returns the token‑quota‑exceeded error JSON:

{ "error": { "message": "Token quota exceeded. You have consumed the maximum number of tokens allowed per minute for your tier.", "type": "quota_error", "code": 429 } }

This response is defined in the token‑quota‑exceeded error description.

For Tier A the request limit (800) is not exceeded (100 < 800) and token usage (100 × 700 = 70 000) stays below its token quota (80 000), so Tier A can handle the load. Tier B also stays within its request limit (100 < 1 200) but its token usage (70 000) exceeds its token quota (60 000). The token quota is hit after 86 requests (86 × 700 = 60 200 > 60 000). When the token quota is exceeded MaaS returns the token‑quota error JSON:
{{ "error": {{ "message": "Token quota exceeded. You have consumed the maximum number of tokens allowed per minute for your tier.", "type": "quota_error", "code": 429 }} }}
Thus Tier B fails first on the token‑quota limit, while Tier A succeeds.

Per minute the token budget allows 100 000 / 120 ≈ 833 requests. The request‑rate limit permits 1 000 requests, so the token quota is the tighter constraint. Over 20 minutes the tier can process 833 × 20 = 16 660 requests. After that the token quota is exceeded first, and MaaS returns the token‑quota‑exceeded JSON error:
{{
  "error": {{
    "message": "Token quota exceeded. You have consumed the maximum number of tokens allowed per minute for your tier.",
    "type": "quota_error",
    "code": 429
  }}
}}

Tier definitions are sourced from the tier‑to‑group‑mapping ConfigMap (chunk 5ca553eb). The dashboard simply reflects the ConfigMap; therefore the numeric **Level** stored in the ConfigMap is authoritative. The assignment algorithm then selects the tier with the highest level among matching entries (chunk 88e6b2a6). Because the ConfigMap defines the “premium” tier at level 1, that level is used, and the dashboard‑shown level 2 is ignored unless the ConfigMap is updated. Consequently the tier resolves with level 1.
{{
  "effectiveTier": "premium",
  "effectiveLevel": 1
}}

The user exceeds the **request‑rate limit** (800 > 500) before the token quota (800 × 100 = 80 000 ≤ 100 000). The MaaS API will return the request‑rate‑limit error JSON:

\{ "error": \{ "message": "Rate limit exceeded. You have exceeded the maximum number of requests per minute for your tier.", "type": "rate_limit_error", "code": 429 \} \}

The tier’s **resource name** (the internal identifier) is immutable after creation; it cannot be changed later. This property is documented as unchangeable once the tier exists.
